// RUN: %not %bezelc -c %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
//
// (`%not` = LLVM's exit-status inverter; the compile is expected to fail — see
// tests/lit/README.md for why every reject test wraps `bezelc` in `not`.)
//
// Issue #1653 — enforce Dispose()-requires-owned-receiver (spec §28.6 "Who may
// call Dispose()", v0.184).
//
// Spec §28.6 (anchor sec-who-may-dispose): explicit disposal — `x.Dispose()`
// and the `?.`/`!.` spellings — requires a receiver the disposer OWNS. A
// `.Dispose()` through a BORROW is a compile error. This test uses the
// canonical genuine borrow: a non-`owned` PARAMETER. `Close(r: Resource)` gets
// `r` as a borrow — the CALLER retains ownership — so `r.Dispose()` would
// dispose a value the caller still owns (a double-dispose). §28.6 lists "a
// borrowed local or parameter" as a rejected receiver.
//
// (The rule is enforced only for genuine-borrow receiver FORMS the ownership
// pass can identify by symbol resolution — a non-`owned` parameter, `this`, a
// non-`owned` field read, a catch/pattern binding, an IEnumerable/enumerator-
// sourced iteration variable, and an owning-collection element. A borrow held
// in a plain LOCAL is a recorded gap: the ownership pass classifies an
// enumerator/subscription local — both disposable — identically to a
// borrow-holding local, so rejecting on that would over-reject. See the
// checkDisposeReceiverOwnership gap note.)
//
// THE STRUCTURAL POINT (same as ref_out_owned_local_1682.bz): this rejection is
// emitted by the OWNERSHIP pass, which runs after sema — a sema-only harness
// never observes it (the #1682 lesson). lit invokes the REAL `bezelc`, which
// always runs the full pipeline, so the §28.6 diagnostic is always in the
// observed output.
module M;
class Resource public method Ping(): () end Ping; end Resource;
class Program
  public static method Close(r: Resource): ()
    r.Dispose();
  end Close;
  public static method Main(): int return 0; end Main;
end Program;

// The ownership pass names the offending category ("parameter `r` ... a
// borrow") and the rule (§28.6). bezelc emits diagnostics as
// `file:line:col: message` with no "error:" severity word, so we anchor on the
// `:line:col:` shape and the rule text.
// CHECK: :{{[0-9]+}}:{{[0-9]+}}: cannot call `Dispose()` on parameter `r`: a non-`owned` parameter is a borrow
// CHECK-SAME: (§28.6
